A Moth project is the directory you select. That directory is both the build root and the source root. There is no implicit `src/` folder.

### Starter config

Config uses two ordinary named records:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    project #= (
        name = "my_site",
    )

    html #= ()
]

`name` is optional and falls back to the selected folder name. There are no `$project` or `$html_builder` directives.

### Where things live

- `config.moth` at the selected root is optional and controls build settings.
- Everything below the selected root is source unless it is excluded or is generated output.
- `_dev/` and `_release/` are generated output. Do not edit them.
- A folder name starting with `_` is excluded from the build unless you select it directly.

### Up to three root files per module folder

Each source folder can contain `@mod.moth`, `@package.moth` and `@entry.moth`. Any subset works. They form one module with shared helper files and separate root scopes.

- `@mod.moth` exports the normal API.
- `@package.moth` exports a scoped support API, or the project package API at the project root.
- `@entry.moth` holds page code and document metadata.

A page appears for every folder with `@entry.moth`. The file can be empty, hold only metadata or hold runtime code:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    #[$in title:Welcome]
]

That metadata-only entry still produces a page. No other filename creates a page.

### Shared scoped packages

An `@package.moth` root creates a scoped support surface. For example, `shared/@package.moth` creates `@shared`. Its owner module, normal sibling modules and their descendants may depend on it. Other support packages in the same scope and the package's private implementation subtree cannot.

When a folder has both an `@mod.moth` and an `@package.moth`, helpers are shared while each root keeps its own declarations and public interface. A project-root `@package.moth` is the external project package surface. Internal modules cannot import it.

### Outputs are generated

The build command creates hidden output folders. They are not source. The builder cleans up stale artefacts automatically.
